{"chapter": 7, "sha256": "46c723c49d661daa138b01d2559e5f300ff66566a436f12c326b61ad0cf5e145", "voice": {"voice_id": "ppLqTilh7rH7fbUVlXsf", "name": "David - Documentary", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.55, "similarity_boost": 0.8, "style": 0.15, "use_speaker_boost": true}}, "source": "docs/script.md", "files": {"narration": {"file": "ch7.mp3", "text": "About 12,900 years ago, as the last Ice Age was ending, the warming stopped. For more than a thousand years the northern world fell back into cold. Geologists call it the Younger Dryas. Hancock cites about a hundred peer-reviewed studies arguing that fragments of a comet set it off. At Abu Hureyra, an early village in Syria, their authors report glass melted at more than 2,200 degrees.\n\nThen, as the cold lifts, a monument appears on a hilltop in south-east Turkey. Göbekli Tepe is a set of great rings of T-shaped stone pillars, carved with animals. It was built around 9500 BCE, seven thousand years before the Great Pyramid. Its excavators say the people who built it were still hunters and foragers. They had no farms, no pottery and no metal. Its first excavator, Klaus Schmidt, believed they later filled it in on purpose.\n\nSchoch now points to Göbekli Tepe when he is asked where the older civilisation was. For this piece too, it is what makes the rest worth taking seriously. If people who did not farm could raise this, then a plan laid out at Giza in the eleventh millennium BCE is not absurd. It is a question of evidence.\n\nSome of that evidence has never been gathered.", "seconds": 90.7, "alignment": {"characters": ["A", "b", "o", "u", "t", " ", "1", "2", ",", "9", "0", "0", " ", "y", "e", "a", "r", "s", " ", "a", "g", "o", ",", " ", "a", "s", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "l", "a", "s", "t", " ", "I", "c", "e", " ", "A", "g", "e", " ", "w", "a", "s", " ", "e", "n", "d", "i", "n", "g", ",", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "w", "a", "r", "m", "i", "n", "g", " ", "s", "t", "o", "p", "p", 